	<annotations>
		<text id="0" type="text/markdown" rect="(215.66666666666669, 169.22222222222223, 158.0, 54.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">**EDA = Explorative Data Analysis**</text>
		<text id="1" type="text/plain" rect="(896.6666666666667, 364.4444444444444, 201.0, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">works on single column</text>
		<text id="2" type="text/plain" rect="(895.6666666666666, 469.22222222222223, 239.0, 54.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">works on a single column</text>
		<text id="3" type="text/plain" rect="(892.6666666666666, 591.2222222222222, 249.0, 33.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">works on 2+ columns</text>
		<arrow id="4" start="(703.6666666666666, 971.2222222222222)" end="(628.6666666666666, 966.2222222222222)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="5" type="text/markdown" rect="(727.6666666666666, 938.2222222222222, 293.0, 138.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">it displays the **correlation matrix** by pressing the RUN button on the bottom-left hand corner</text>
		<text id="6" type="text/markdown" rect="(21.777777777777786, 830.6666666666665, 264.0, 201.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">An example of **positive** correlation (from 0 to 1): between the price and the square meters of an apartment.
An example of **negative** correlation (from 0 to -1): between the price and the kilometers of an used car. The threshold for importance of correlationis |0.7|</text>
		<arrow id="7" start="(292.11111111111114, 919.1111111111109)" end="(402.4444444444445, 968.1111111111109)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="8" type="text/plain" rect="(342.6666666666667, 1043.2222222222222, 292.0, 96.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">In EDA all columns play the same role (feature ; there are NOT targets and predictors (later when building predictive models)</text>
		<text id="9" type="text/markdown" rect="(-311.6666666666667, 387.3333333333333, 231.0, 453.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">We can look at **metadata** (**data type** and **role**) and change them (--&gt; they appear in bold).&lt;br&gt;
The CSV indeed stores only values; everything else lives in what Orange calls the Domain: the object describing each variable's name, type, role and (for categoricals) the list of values. When the File widget reads credit.csv, it builds that Domain on the fly: first auto-detection guesses types, then your manual overrides are applied on top. The bold rows are exactly those overrides — columns where you departed from what auto-detection guessed (the Reset button next to the list reverts them).</text>
		<arrow id="10" start="(-233.0, 388.7777777777776)" end="(-233.1111111111111, 339.33333333333326)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<arrow id="11" start="(729.6666666666666, 220.2222222222222)" end="(789.6666666666666, 245.2222222222222)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="12" type="text/markdown" rect="(537.6666666666667, 178.22222222222223, 202.0, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">We look at **data** and we cannot change them (it's just visualization)</text>
		<text id="13" type="text/markdown" rect="(1154.7777777777778, 160.22222222222223, 405.0, 285.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">there's no single "edit the data" widget in Orange — and that's by design. Orange treats every change as a workflow step: a widget receives data on its input, transforms it, and sends the result downstream, and any change in one widget is instantly propagated to all widgets connected after it. So instead of editing cells in place (the way you would in Excel), you insert a widget that performs the modification, and the original data stays untouched — which is exactly what makes workflows reproducible. The Data Table widget, in particular, is read-only: you can select rows in it and send the selection onward, but you can't type new values into cells. (*Claude.ai*)</text>
		<arrow id="14" start="(1131.6666666666665, 171.22222222222226)" end="(872.6666666666666, 238.22222222222229)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<arrow id="15" start="(49.222222222222236, 210.3333333333334)" end="(48.7777777777778, 248.55555555555566)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="16" type="text/markdown" rect="(-8.222222222222229, 155.11111111111114, 132.22222222222223, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">to place **filters** on the columns</text>
		<arrow id="17" start="(120.00000000000003, 384.44444444444446)" end="(190.00000000000003, 311.11111111111114)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="18" type="text/markdown" rect="(-10.0, 390.00000000000006, 152.22222222222223, 96.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">to **split columns** into one of4 groups (feature, target, meta, ignored)</text>
		<arrow id="19" start="(-203.33333333333326, 1026.6666666666665)" end="(-134.4444444444444, 972.2222222222222)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="20" type="text/plain" rect="(-227.77777777777777, 1254.4444444444446, 365.5555555555556, 138.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">Are the two computed columns Xand Y correlated? How much? Yes, because ML crimes and SOS come from independent sources (Police / Judges and Banks) that means that most of SOS (from banks) were RIGHT, ie, they then matched with a ML crime discovered by Police)</text>
		<arrow id="21" start="(-54.44444444444446, 1245.5555555555557)" end="(2.2222222222222356, 1174.4444444444443)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="22" type="text/markdown" rect="(-317.77777777777777, 1035.5555555555554, 240.0, 180.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">The first 4 columns are the original ones from Italian central Authority (in 2015), X and Y were subsequently computed (they are just ML Crimes and SOS operations **normalized** wrt the number of branches in the regions)</text>
		<text id="23" type="text/markdown" rect="(1071.1111111111109, 1043.3333333333333, 243.33333333333326, 101.22222222222217)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">The line plot is a very important tool to display **temporal data**. Credit dataset has NO temporal data.</text>
		<arrow id="24" start="(1344.4444444444446, 777.7777777777778)" end="(1358.8888888888887, 865.5555555555555)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="25" type="text/markdown" rect="(1171.1111111111104, 689.9999999999998, 280.0000000000009, 141.0000000000001)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">We can see an **increasing trend** over the years and  a **seasonal pattern** (top in winter, bottom in summer). Why?</text>
		<text id="26" type="text/markdown" rect="(128.8888888888889, 1083.3333333333335, 224.44444444444449, 68.8888888888888)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">&lt;u&gt;CORRELATIONS&lt;/u&gt;</text>
		<text id="27" type="text/markdown" rect="(1120.0, 545.5555555555557, 125.55555555555543, 83.33333333333331)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">&lt;u&gt;PLOTS&lt;/u&gt;</text>
		<text id="28" type="text/markdown" rect="(-334.44444444444446, 0.0, 256.6666666666667, 62.22222222222223)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">The **typical flow in Machine Learning** is the following:
- EDA
- Data pre-processing
- Predictive Models (regression)</text>
		<text id="29" type="text/markdown" rect="(433.3333333333337, 1227.7777777777785, 477.7777777777778, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">**DATA PRE-PROCESSING**: **standardization**; identify/manage MVs; identify/manage outliers.</text>
		<text id="30" type="text/plain" rect="(913.3333333333334, 1315.5555555555557, 380.0, 159.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">We need standardization for at least three main reasons:
- compare data in parallel
- compute distances
</text>
		<text id="31" type="text/plain" rect="(924.6666666666667, 1456.5555555555557, 233.33333333333326, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">MVs can:
- alter statistics
- confuse models
- distort your decisions</text>
		<arrow id="32" start="(-124.0, 68.0)" end="(-70.0, 69.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="33" type="text/markdown" rect="(-44.0, 10.0, 588.0, 159.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">you will be able to apply this template to YOUR own file (with few modifications), not necessarily a customer file.
One limitation:
- Orange can manage up to **10^4 rows** (no big data!)
- BUT (good news): if your csv dataset is larger (10^5 - 10^6 (miliions rows) or even 10^7 (tenths million rows, you can **sample** from the population.</text>
		<text id="34" type="text/plain" rect="(-180.66666666666674, 178.44444444444434, 153.33333333333337, 75.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">You can sample from your csv file if too large (&gt; 10^4)</text>
		<arrow id="35" start="(604.0, 1709.9999999999998)" end="(543.0, 1710.0)" fill="#C1272D" />
		<text id="36" type="text/plain" rect="(620.0, 1678.0, 136.0, 96.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">Visualization of Missing Values</text>
		<text id="37" type="text/plain" rect="(806.0, 1731.0, 427.0, 159.0)" font-family="Segoe UI" font-size="16">Outliers (ie, values exceptionally high or low in that column) need to be identified and managed, for many reasons
- can alter statistics
- can affect models (even strongly)</text>
	</annotations>
